00:41:00.599 --> 00:41:05.355
Alright, let's, uh, Josh, we have a very special, uh, portion of our show.
好了，我们，呃，乔伊，我们节目有一个非常特别的，呃，环节。

00:41:05.355 --> 00:41:07.347
Would you like to introduce it?
你想介绍一下吗？

00:41:07.347 --> 00:41:07.925
Oh, sure.
哦，好啊。

00:41:07.925 --> 00:41:09.724
Yeah, this is very exciting.
对，这非常令人兴奋。

00:41:09.724 --> 00:41:20.841
This is, uh, a section we like to call questions and observations from a clinical psychologist who's never seen how I met your mother and also happens to be married to Josh.
这是，呃，一个我们喜欢称之为“来自一位临床心理学家的提问与观察”的板块，她从来没看过《老爸老妈浪漫史》，而且刚好是乔什的妻子。

00:41:20.841 --> 00:41:30.737
So I was most taken by that moment when Ted came to the conclusion based on Marshall's advice that love shouldn't be that hard, should be simple and easy.
所以泰德根据马歇尔的建议得出结论，认为爱情不应该那么难，应该简单轻松，那个时刻最触动我。

00:41:30.737 --> 00:41:39.412
And I'm so often working with patients around this concept, what should it feel like when you found the person you're meant to be with?
我和患者共事时，也经常探讨这个概念：当你找到命中注定要在一起的那个人时，感觉应该是什么样的？

00:41:39.412 --> 00:41:41.211
What parts should feel easy?
哪些部分应该让人觉得轻松？

00:41:41.211 --> 00:41:45.452
And when is it okay and actually normal for it to feel quite hard?
什么时候觉得挺难的其实也是正常的、可以接受的？

00:41:45.452 --> 00:41:49.693
Because, you know, in truth, long-term love does indeed take work.
因为，你知道，事实上，长期的爱确实需要经营。

00:41:49.693 --> 00:41:52.200
And how do you know when it's too hard?
又该如何判断什么时候是“太难了”呢？

00:41:53.320 --> 00:42:06.840
So these are very big questions, but I am wondering where you stand here, you know, more specifically, what is easy and what's the kind of hard that just is the price of admission for a long-term relationship?
所以这些都是很大的问题，但我很好奇你的看法，更具体地说，什么是轻松的，而哪种困难又是长期恋爱必不可少的代价？

00:42:07.800 --> 00:42:08.925
That's a good one.
这问题问得好。

00:42:08.925 --> 00:42:11.801
I found myself thinking about it too, Jernana.
我也在思考这个问题，杰娜娜（Jernana）。

00:42:11.801 --> 00:42:19.803
When you're watching, when Marshall gives that speech, I hadn't watched that speech in a long time and it felt very young to me.
当你看着、当马歇尔发表那段演讲时，我很久没看那段演讲了，觉得当时的自己非常年轻。

00:42:19.803 --> 00:42:29.244
Marshall is the older, wiser guy in that moment because he's engaged and he's been with Lilly since he's 18 years old from freshman year of of college.
那一刻的马歇尔是个更成熟、更有智慧的人，因为他订婚了，而且从18岁大一就开始和莉莉在一起了。

00:42:29.244 --> 00:42:36.246
And yet, looking back at it now, it's like, that is kind of a young take in a way, because it's not easy, right?
然而，现在回过头来看，这多少有点年轻人的视角，因为事情并不轻松，对吧？

00:42:36.246 --> 00:42:36.809
It's not.
确实不轻松。

00:42:36.809 --> 00:42:38.872
It's like you have to work at it.
这就好像你必须为之付出努力。

00:42:38.872 --> 00:42:40.998
It's nothing is always effortless.
没有什么是永远毫不费力的。

00:42:40.998 --> 00:42:45.750
I think he was talking about, there has to be some spirit of effortlessness.
我想他当时说的是，必须有一种毫不费力的精神。

00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:50.255
There has to be some effortlessness to the decision to be with that person, right?
对于和那个人在一起的这个决定，必须得有一种不费吹灰之力般的笃定，对吧？

00:42:50.255 --> 00:42:51.409
That's how I took it.
我是这么理解的。

00:42:51.409 --> 00:42:54.157
It should be like, well, I have to breathe oxygen.
这应该就像，好吧，我需要呼吸氧气。

00:42:54.157 --> 00:42:55.365
That's not negotiable.
这是不容协商的。

00:42:55.365 --> 00:42:57.234
It should kind of feel that right?
感觉应该差不多是这样，对吧？

00:42:57.234 --> 00:42:58.662
Love should feel that way.
爱情就该是这种感觉。

00:42:58.662 --> 00:43:05.915
It should feel worth, I guess it should be an effort, this decision, and then beyond that, you know, there's going to be hard stuff.
我觉得，这个决定本身应该是值得的、毫不费力的，而在那之后，你知道，会有艰难的事情。

00:43:05.915 --> 00:43:09.157
But the decision part should feel like it's natural to you.
但做出决定的那部分应该让你觉得顺理成章。

00:43:09.157 --> 00:43:12.069
And I think that's how I viewed the speech this time.
我想这就是我这次看待那段演讲的方式。

00:43:12.070 --> 00:43:13.088
How did you feel that, Josh?
乔什，你当时是怎么觉得的？

00:43:13.088 --> 00:43:14.070
How did you interpret that?
你是怎么解读的？

00:43:14.070 --> 00:43:15.853
Yeah, I think that's right.
对，我觉得没错。

00:43:15.853 --> 00:43:33.750
I mean, I think, especially in first season, Marshall and Lily do have a, they're, I mean, because they're, they're, especially in this first season, they're more, they are the parents, they're like Ted's parents, you know, they are, that really is a role that they play.
我是说，我觉得，特别是在第一季里，马歇尔和莉莉确实有种……我的意思是，因为他们，特别是在第一季里，他们更像是父母，就像泰德的父母一样，你知道，这确实是他们扮演的角色。

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:40.619
And even Lily, this is a drum roll line, but she says, to Robin, like, happiness shouldn't be this hard.
甚至莉莉——这里有个高潮台词——她对罗宾说，幸福不应该这么难。

00:43:40.619 --> 00:43:42.996
Doesn't she say something like that?
她是不是说了类似的话？

00:43:42.996 --> 00:43:43.591
She does.
她确实说了。

00:43:43.591 --> 00:43:44.091
Yeah.
对。

00:43:44.091 --> 00:43:45.412
Which is both great.
这番话既很棒。

00:43:45.412 --> 00:43:50.629
And also, like a little bit condescending and a little, like not entirely true.
同时又有点居高临下，还有点，并非完全真实。

00:43:50.629 --> 00:43:51.129
Yeah.
对。

00:43:50.789 --> 00:43:57.110
I think some people struggle in their career very mightily, some people struggle in relationships.
我觉得有些人事业上举步维艰，有些人则在感情中苦苦挣扎。

00:43:57.110 --> 00:44:07.690
But I don't think that anything we do as humans, I don't think that anyone can inoculate themselves against struggle and hardship.
但我不认为人类做的任何事、我不认为任何人能让自己对挣扎和苦难免疫。

00:44:07.690 --> 00:44:10.630
I think it's coming for all of us in various forms.
我觉得各种形式的苦难终究会降临到我们所有人头上。

00:44:10.630 --> 00:44:23.995
And I think when it comes to relationships, a friend of mine who's older than me, but very wise about relationships, he said, you know, the thing that makes us fall and love is this feeling of similarity.
而谈到亲密关系时，我的一位年纪比我大、但在感情上非常有智慧的朋友说过，你知道，让我们坠入爱河的东西，是这种相似感。

00:44:23.995 --> 00:44:38.671
Like, oh my God, we love the same bands and the same movies and we have the same taste and aesthetic and, you know, and we both love to talk about our feeling, like all these things that go check, check, check, check, check.
就像，天哪，我们喜欢同样的乐队、同样的电影，我们有相同的品味和审美，而且，你知道，我们都喜欢谈论感受，所有这些都完美契合，打钩、打钩、打钩、打钩。

00:44:38.671 --> 00:44:40.047
Oh, don't we line up?
哦，我们难道不是天作之合吗？

00:44:40.047 --> 00:44:44.437
And that's, it's almost like nature's way to get you bonded, right?
这几乎就像是大自然让你把彼此绑在一起的方式，对吧？

00:44:44.437 --> 00:44:50.989
And then after you've been with a person for a while, these differences start announcing themselves.
然后当你和一个人相处了一段时间后，这些差异就开始显现出来了。

00:44:50.989 --> 00:45:17.786
And then you have to navigate The the difference, you know, I don't think Jordy, I don't want to mind me saying this but like a central thing in our relationship is like She is a Google calendar planner in a way that would make your head spin like she'll go Hey, do you want to do so-and-so next Thursday at three and I'll go Okay, and I'll look down on my phone and I already I have a Google calendar invite.
接着你必须去处理这些差异，你知道，乔迪，我介意你这么说吗——比如我们关系中的一个核心问题是：她是一个谷歌日历规划狂魔，程度会让你头晕目眩。比如她会说：“嘿，你下周四三点想去干吗干吗吗？”然后我会说：“好吧。”接着我低头看手机，发现自己已经收到一个谷歌日历邀请了。

00:45:17.786 --> 00:45:21.389
It's there down, it's just she leaned it into my phone.
它就在那儿，她直接同步到我手机里了。

00:45:21.389 --> 00:45:30.389
And I have a thing where I don't know how I'm going to feel at Thursday on Thursday at three because I'm not thinking about Thursday at three, I'm thinking about today.
而我有个毛病，我不知道自己周四三点会是什么心情，因为我没在想周四三点的事，我在想今天的事。

00:45:30.389 --> 00:45:31.869
That's future Josh's problem.
那是“未来乔什”的麻烦。

00:45:31.869 --> 00:45:34.510
It's future Josh's problem, it's not my problem.
那是“未来乔什”的麻烦，不是我的麻烦。

00:45:34.510 --> 00:45:42.469
And I think that, you know, I don't know if this is so gendered because I think there are men who are incredibly regimented and planning and list making and all those things.
而且我认为，我不知道这是否带有性别色彩，因为我觉得有些男人也极具条理性、喜欢规划、列清单以及所有这些事情。

00:45:42.469 --> 00:45:50.309
But I have had to yield and bend more towards, Like, I've really had to like get on top of the Google shared calendar, right?
但我不得不向现实妥协并做出更多调整，比如，我真的不得不开始使用共享的谷歌日历，对吧？

00:45:50.309 --> 00:45:55.670
Like, like for the sake of my marriage, I've had to become much more of a planner.
为了我的婚姻，我不得不变得更有规划性。

00:45:55.670 --> 00:46:01.949
And planning vacations is very hard because I'm like, I don't know how many of you, I don't know how I'll have a job then, you know, I'm talking about our marriage.
而且规划假期非常难，因为我想，我不知道你们有多少人……我不知道到那时候我还有没有工作呢（我是指我们的婚姻）。

00:46:01.949 --> 00:46:02.590
I know.
我懂。

00:46:03.750 --> 00:46:05.789
Did she walk in right as you began this page?
她是不是刚好在你开始说这段话的时候走进来了？

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:09.909
So everyone, it's been a real navigation.
所以各位，这真是一段不断磨合的过程。

00:46:09.909 --> 00:46:15.349
And it's also like, you know, she's had to bend and yield in certain ways towards, and I've had to bend and yield.
而且这也是，你知道的，她在某些方面也必须做出妥协和调整，而我也必须妥协和调整。

00:46:15.349 --> 00:46:48.529
but that's the kind of thing where, you know, that's like good struggle, like that's like good, you know, and it also I think part of the blessing and opportunity of a relationship is it dislodges you from your patterns or at least highlights them to be seen, which sometimes is you don't want them to be highlighted seem like, you know, but it ultimately makes you a more elastic kind of person, right?
但这就是那种，你知道的，好的挣扎，就像是那种好的，你知道的，而且我也认为亲密关系的祝福与机遇之一，就是它能打破你的固有模式，或者至少把它们暴露出来让你看见——虽然有时你并不希望它们被看见、感觉像是在被审视，但它最终会让你成为一个更有韧性的人，对吧？

00:46:48.529 --> 00:46:52.070
And which I think is a net positive, right?
而我认为这是一个绝对的积极因素，对吧？

00:46:52.070 --> 00:46:52.948
It's a net positive.
这是一个绝对的积极因素。

00:46:52.948 --> 00:46:54.090
You have to evolve, right?
你必须进化，对吧？

00:46:54.090 --> 00:46:59.140
You can't get with someone and it's all a yes and you need to be challenged a little bit or else you'll never grow.
你不能和一个人在一起，然后凡事都只有“好啊”，你必须受到一些挑战，否则你永远不会成长。

00:46:59.140 --> 00:47:07.264
Even this episode, it's funny, the storyline, the little B, I wouldn't even call it a B, but a C or D story runner like is Lily and Marshall not agreeing on anything about this wedding.
甚至这一集，很有趣，这条主线剧情，这个B线剧情——我甚至不该叫它B线，应该算C线或D线剧情跑马灯——就是莉莉和马歇尔在这个婚礼的任何事情上都意见不合。

00:47:07.264 --> 00:47:18.682
And again, to come back to the gender flip idea, Marshall wants this much sort of like, you know, Lily says, oh, you've been dreaming of, you know, Marshall's like, I'm dreaming about wedding ever since I was and she's like a little girl, you know what I mean?
再说一次，回到性别对调的想法上，马歇尔想要这种非常传统的、花里胡哨的婚礼，莉莉却说：“哦，你一直梦想着……”马歇尔就说：“我从小到大一直梦想着办婚礼……”而她就像个小女孩一样，你知道我的意思吗？

00:47:18.682 --> 00:47:20.219
There's a little gender flip there.
这里有点性别对调。

00:47:20.219 --> 00:47:23.029
Marshall wants this way more traditional sort of fruity wedding.
马歇尔比她更渴望这种非常传统、花哨的婚礼。

00:47:23.029 --> 00:47:26.323
And she clearly wants something a little less traditional out in the woods.
而她明显想要一些不那么传统、在树林里办的婚礼。

00:47:26.323 --> 00:47:29.397
And like, this whole episode's storyline is them not agreeing on shit.
而且整集的故事线就是他俩在各种破事上意见不合。

00:47:29.397 --> 00:47:33.349
And yet he's still his overall feeling that he says to Stewart is it should be effortless.
然而他对他向斯图尔特表达的整体感受仍然是：这应该是不费吹灰之力的。

00:47:33.349 --> 00:47:43.349
And I think he's talking about the decision to go through this thing called life with this other person, even as he's at the same time, not agreeing on any one thing with Stuart.
我想他说的应该是和另一个人一起走过这段名为人生的旅程这个决定本身，尽管与此同时他在任何一件事上都跟斯图尔特——我是说跟莉莉——意见不一致。

00:47:43.349 --> 00:47:52.269
I mean, with Lilly, he's still able to say this to Stuart, and you see, when they finally got to the wedding, there's like this little moment where they agree on one thing about the wedding planning.
他仍然能对斯图尔特说出这样的话，而且你看，当他们最终走到婚礼现场时，有那么一个小瞬间，他们在婚礼筹备的一件事情上达成了一致。

00:47:52.269 --> 00:47:55.510
But that's not, that's not Marshall and Lilly being in lockstep, you know?
但这并不意味着马歇尔和莉莉总是步调一致，你知道的？

00:47:55.510 --> 00:47:56.510
Right, right.
对，对。

00:47:56.510 --> 00:48:06.590
Also, though, I think in some ways, I don't think like in later seasons, like it doesn't It seemed like Lily Aldrin would want an outdoor way.
不过话说回来，我觉得在某些方面，我觉得在后来的剧集里，莉莉·奥尔德林看起来才不会想要什么户外婚礼呢。

00:48:06.590 --> 00:48:10.710
I know, we met a little bit of a little bit of a little like hippie for her.
我知道，我们遇到的这个版本对她来说有点太……有点太嬉皮士了。

00:48:10.710 --> 00:48:11.710
Like I don't know.
我是说，不知道。

00:48:11.710 --> 00:48:12.710
A little hippie.
有点嬉皮士。

00:48:12.710 --> 00:48:13.710
Yeah.
对。

00:48:13.710 --> 00:48:14.710
Yeah.
对。

00:48:14.710 --> 00:48:19.949
Like I feel like Marshall would want like a Minnesota, like the high school football field in Minnesota or something.
我觉得马歇尔反而会想要那种明尼苏达风格的，比如明尼苏达的高中足球场之类的婚礼。

00:48:19.949 --> 00:48:20.949
You could have flipped it there.
你当时是可以把他们对调一下的。

00:48:20.949 --> 00:48:21.949
Yeah.
对。

00:48:21.949 --> 00:48:22.949
You could have flipped it.
你可以对调一下的。

00:48:22.949 --> 00:48:23.949
Yeah, it's true.
对，没错。

00:48:23.949 --> 00:48:24.949
We're still finding the characters.
我们还在摸索这些角色。

00:48:24.949 --> 00:48:36.079
The one thing we need for sure is that Lily's best friend is Claudia, that I'll stand by.
我们唯一可以确定的是，莉莉最好的朋友是克劳迪娅，这一点我坚信不疑。

00:48:36.079 --> 00:48:41.519
Alec, I couldn't help but notice you're wearing a really nice shirt today.
亚历克，我忍不住注意到你今天穿了一件非常好看的衬衫。

00:48:41.519 --> 00:48:42.960
Do you want to talk us through that?
你想跟我们聊聊这个吗？

00:48:42.960 --> 00:48:43.800
Top bro.
顶流兄弟。

00:48:43.800 --> 00:48:44.960
Yeah, top bro.
对，顶流兄弟。

00:48:45.800 --> 00:48:46.639
Oh my God.
噢天哪。

00:48:46.639 --> 00:48:47.000
Okay.
好吧。

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:49.840
And then we know what's going on Instagram now with this whole thing.
然后现在我们都知道Instagram上关于整件事的动态了。

00:48:50.199 --> 00:48:52.239
This is this part of a new March here.
这是新品牌宣传的一部分吗？

00:48:52.239 --> 00:48:54.840
This is our college style.
这就是我们的大学风。

00:48:55.159 --> 00:48:57.280
How we made your how what is the show called?
我们是怎么制作你的……这个节目的名字叫什么来着？

00:48:57.280 --> 00:48:58.519
How we made your mother?
《我们是怎么制作你的母亲》？

00:48:58.559 --> 00:49:00.840
Yeah, that's what's called this shirt.
对，这件衬衫就是这么叫的。

00:49:00.880 --> 00:49:02.000
You're the producer of it.
你是这个节目的制作人。

00:49:02.159 --> 00:49:02.659
Yeah.
对。

00:49:02.659 --> 00:49:03.159
Yeah.
对。

00:49:02.719 --> 00:49:04.360
You work on the show because I was a good show.
你参与了这个节目，因为这是个好节目。

00:49:04.360 --> 00:49:16.119
Um, I don't have the, I should know this because I am the producer, but I believe by the time you're hearing this, we have already been to Wesleyan University and, and, and, and done a little live show there, which will be in our feed.
嗯，我没有……我本该知道这一点的，因为我是制作人，但我相信，当你们听到这段音频时，我们已经去过卫斯理大学，在那里做了一场小型现场秀，这会收录在我们的节目源中。

00:49:16.119 --> 00:49:31.440
And we thought, okay, well, we're going to a college, like a college style shirt, uh, we've got another one that's just our logo, 10% of all the profits are going to, uh, how you make a difference are our, our campaign to support a congenital heart disease research.
我们当时想，好吧，我们要去一所大学，弄一件大学风格的衬衫；我们还有另一款只有我们标志的衬衫，所有利润的10%将用于“如何创造不同”（How You Make a Difference）项目——这是我们支持先天性心脏病研究的活动。

00:49:31.440 --> 00:49:32.679
Very proud of that.
对此我们感到非常自豪。

